Major reproducibility issues identified:The starter notebook depended on the original analyst's environment. Data loaded from a hardcoded Colab path (/content/...), imports not centrally located, and no package versions were documented. The BMI "bootstrap" drew a single unseeded resample, so its result changed on every run. isna() missed missing values encoded as physiologically impossible zeros. Once those zeros were handled, the original t-test returned NaN because it did not exclude missing values, and it assumed equal group variances. LaTeX did not render, and template placeholders (var1–var8, target) were nonspecific and uninformative.

Changes made: I consolidated all imports and a fixed RANDOM_SEED into one setup cell that prints the Python and package versions, pinned those versions in requirements.txt, and documented setup in the README. Data now load through a function that uses a user-specified file first, then a local repository copy, then the public GitHub repository. Validation functions flag problematic data errors that will hamper subsequent analyses, and data cleaning sets implausible values to missing using documented bounds. Every random step is seeded, and the bootstrap now uses 1,000 resamples with a 95% confidence interval. I replaced the t-test with Welch's test, added a Mann–Whitney U test, Spearman correlations, and two bivariate figures, and rewrote the documentation to be dataset-specific.

Reproducibility verification: I ran Restart session and run all twice in Google Colab and confirmed identical outputs, including the bootstrap output. I also confirmed the Colab link opens in a private browser window.